content.thinking:
Perfect! Now I can see that `normalize()` in `src/main/java/org/jsoup/internal/Normalizer.java` does both:
1. `lowerCase()` - converts to lowercase
2. `.trim()` - removes leading/trailing whitespace

The issue is clear now. In the `AttributeKeyPair` constructor:

```java
public AttributeKeyPair(String key, String value, boolean trimQuoted) {
    Validate.notEmpty(key);
    Validate.notEmpty(value);

    this.key = normalize(key);
    boolean quoted = value.startsWith("'") && value.endsWith("'")
        || value.startsWith("\"") && value.endsWith("\"");
    if (quoted)
        value = value.substring(1, value.length() - 1);

    // normalize value based on whether it was quoted and trimQuoted flag
    // keeps whitespace for attribute val starting or ending, when quoted
    if (trimQuoted || !quoted)
        this.value = normalize(value); // lowercase and trims
    else
        this.value = lowerCase(value); // only lowercase
}
```

The problems are:
1. `Validate.notEmpty(value)` is checked BEFORE quotes are removed. For a value like `""` or `''`, this will fail even though after removing quotes it would be valid (empty).
2. For quoted values, when `trimQuoted=true`, it calls `normalize(value)` which trims the value, removing leading/trailing spaces that should be preserved in CSS selectors.
3. `AttributeWithValue` and `AttributeWithValueContaining` don't pass `trimQuoted=false`, so they trim when they shouldn't.

The fix:
1. Remove the early `Validate.notEmpty(value)` check
2. Change `AttributeWithValue` and `AttributeWithValueContaining` to pass `trimQuoted=false` instead of the default `true`


3. Move validation to occur after quote removal and processing, ensuring the key remains non-empty while allowing empty values. I should check the test cases to confirm what behavior is actually expected.

